<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#iyoba-of-benin">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iyoba of Benin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iyoba, Queen Mother</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iyoba, known in English as the Queen Mother, is an important female titleholder in the chieftaincy system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. According to tradition, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a> created the office for his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idia">Idia</a>, who had rallied the Binis to his side in a civil war with his brother and against the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a>. Ranking equal to the senior chiefs, the Iyoba held the town of Uselu as a perpetual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, where she lived after her son's coronation, forbidden to see him again yet serving as his chief advisor. She commanded her own regiment in war, and after death was honoured as her son's patron goddess in the shrine of the kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iyoba-of-benin.jpg" alt="Iyoba of Benin" width="330" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edo people · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Mother_Pendant_Mask-_Iyoba_MET_DP231460.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
